{"id":"c040928e-a51a-4609-a856-ffb74c72d7fa","object":"chat.completion","created":1785023106,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        p = node['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        new_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_new = set()\\n        for rid in new_retained:\\n            symbols_new.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_new):\\n            retained = new_retained\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. The task is SE-PE-01, which involves \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The input payload has nodes (tree nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The output must be an object with removed_node_ids (list of strings) and retained_node_ids (list of strings). The schema says array of strings, but order? It says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in removal order. The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we process nodes in order: deeper depth first, then within same depth, tie-break by ID (probably alphabetical/string comparison). Then for each node in that order, we try to remove it if: it's removable, not root, not a required symbol, and its removal does not cause loss of a required symbol? Also \"retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests we can only remove leaf nodes? But nodes can have children; we might remove a node if all its children are also being removed? Wait: The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies that if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent. So we must remove nodes bottom-up: first remove leaves that are removable and not required, etc. Then after removing a node, its parent may become a leaf (if all children removed), and then can be considered for removal. So the algorithm is: sort nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending (stable tie). For each node in that order, if removable and not root and not required_symbol, and currently it has no children that are retained (i.e., all children are either not present in the tree (i.e., removed) but we might need to track which nodes are currently retained). Also need to check that required symbols remain represented after removal. That means after removal, the set of symbols of all retained nodes must include all required symbols. Since removal is tentative, we check before actually removing. So we need to maintain a set of retained nodes, and after removing a candidate node, check if all required symbols are still present among retained nodes. If yes, remove it. If no, skip.\n\nWe also need to consider \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but here it seems only deletion is considered? Or there might be replacement? The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but then in constraints: \"grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" It seems we just delete nodes that are removable, and ensure grammar validity? Actually \"grammar_valid\" might refer to that the tree after deletion must be a valid derivation from the grammar? But we don't have grammar rules. Possibly the \"symbol\" of each node is a grammar symbol, and the parent-child relationships represent grammar expansions. Deleting a node might break the grammar if the parent still expects that child? But the problem says \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" And \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we must ensure that after removal, the tree still represents a valid parse according to some grammar? But we don't have grammar rules. Maybe the \"grammar_valid\" is automatically satisfied if we only remove nodes that are removable and ensure required symbols remain? Or maybe there is a hidden constraint: the tree is valid only if each node's symbol corresponds to a production that matches its children's symbols? The input doesn't give grammar productions. So likely we just follow the explicit rules: never remove root, required symbol, node with retained children; ensure required symbols remain; process deeper first, tie-break by ID.\n\nAlso \"interestingness_preserved\" is satisfied by checking required_symbols remain. \"registered_candidate_order\" is the order we consider candidates: deeper first, then ID.\n\nSo we need to implement solve(case) that does this.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Input: case (payload) with 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list, maybe '_case_nonce' (ignore).\n- Build a map from node_id to node object. Also build parent-child relationships: for each node, store its children IDs list (initially empty, then fill). For each node, if parent is not None, add node id to parent's children.\n- Determine initial retained set: all node ids.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID ascending (string compare). This gives processing order.\n- For each node in that order:\n  - Check conditions: node['removable'] == True, node['id'] != 'root', node['symbol'] not in required_symbols? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means if the node's symbol is one of the required symbols, we cannot remove it regardless of other conditions. So check that.\n  - Also check that the node currently has no retained children: i.e., for each child_id in children[node['id']], that child_id is still in retained set. If any child is retained, skip.\n  - Then tentatively remove: create a temporary retained set = current retained set minus this node's id. Check if the set of symbols of retained nodes (after removal) still contains all required_symbols. If yes, then actually remove: update retained set to the temporary one, and add node['id'] to removed list (in order). If no, skip.\n- After processing all nodes, the retained set is final. Then produce sorted retained node IDs (sorted ascending string) and removed node IDs in the order they were removed.\n\nNote: The initial retained set includes all nodes. When we remove a node, it's removed permanently; later when considering other nodes, we need to check children retained based on current retained set.\n\nEdge cases: Nodes with parent None are root. We should never remove root. Also nodes with symbol that is required: we must not remove them.\n\nThe payload fixture shows nodes with depth, utility (ignored), removable flag.\n\nAlso note that required_symbols may include 'root' (root symbol). That means root cannot be removed anyway.\n\nImplement solve(case) returning dict with keys removed_node_ids, retained_node_ids.\n\nWe need to ensure we use only Python standard library, no randomness, etc.\n\nNow produce the response: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a string containing Python source.\n\nWe need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes for JSON encoding.\n\nLet's write the Python code.\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        p = node['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(node['id'])\n    \n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_order = []\n    \n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check no retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # tentatively remove\n        new_retained = retained - {nid}\n        # check required symbols still present\n        symbols_new = set()\n        for rid in new_retained:\n            symbols_new.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_new):\n            retained = new_retained\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nBut we also need to consider that root is not removable (it has removable false in examples), but we already check removable flag. Also root's symbol is 'root' which is in required_symbols, so it won't be removed.\n\nAlso we need to ensure that we never remove a node that has retained children. That check is correct.\n\nPotential nuance: The rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" might apply even if the node is required? But required symbol already prevents removal. So fine.\n\nNow produce JSON string with escaped characters. We'll write the entire response.\n\nMake sure to not include any extra text. The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\".\n\nI'll now craft the output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":2262,"total_tokens":4319,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1920},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}